@page "/collections"

<PageTitle>Nested collections</PageTitle>
<h1>Nested collections</h1>
<p>Errors on this page are row-stable: each one stays with its row, in a list of teams that each hold a list of members. Move or remove a row and its messages go with it, because a message follows the row's object, not its place in the list.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Roster.cs", "Collections.razor", "Collections.razor.cs" })">
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Look at the labels before typing anything.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> an asterisk beside every <strong>Name</strong> and every <strong>Alias</strong>.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Team name is required" on the second team, "Alias is required" under each empty <strong>Alias</strong>, and all three in the error summary.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Move team up</strong> on the second team.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the team moves to the top with its two messages, and its entries lead the error summary.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> The error summary lists its entries in the order their fields sit on the page.</div></li>
            <li>Click the <strong>Remove</strong> under the empty <strong>Alias</strong> in team "Alpha".<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the row and its message go, and its entry leaves the error summary, with no second submit.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> The form notices the row leave and re-checks the whole form on its own.</div></li>
            <li>Click the <strong>Remove</strong> under the other team's only <strong>Alias</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Every team needs at least one member" above that team's empty list of members and in the error summary.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Add member</strong> on that team, fill in every <strong>Name</strong> and <strong>Alias</strong>, then click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Submitted" in the status line.</div></li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Teams</strong>: at least one team.</li>
            <li><strong>Name</strong> (each team): required.</li>
            <li><strong>Members</strong> (each team): at least one member.</li>
            <li><strong>Alias</strong> (each member): required.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <HowItWorks>
        <p>Formidable keeps each message with its row's object, not the row's index in the list. Each component on a row names its field through that row's own object, as in <code>For="() =&gt; team.Name"</code>, so a moved row keeps its messages and a removed row takes them with it.</p>
        <p>The page's markup has to move with the object too, so each row is keyed by its object (<code>@@key="team"</code>, <code>@@key="member"</code>). Without the key, Blazor would reuse each row's components by position. After a move, a row's boxes would show the moved team's values, while its messages, and the field its buttons report to, still belonged to the team that first sat in that place.</p>
        <p>The error summary lists its entries in the order their fields sit on the page, so moving a team up moves its entries up too. <strong>Move up</strong> moves a member within its team the same way, messages and all.</p>
        <p>Each list sits inside a <code>FormidableField</code> (the component that wraps one field's markup and tells the markup inside it which field it is). The buttons edit the lists through methods that field hands its markup: <code>AddItem</code>, <code>RemoveItem</code>, and <code>TryEdit</code> for a move.</p>
        <p>Each of those calls reports a change to the list, so the form checks it as you edit. That is why removing a team's last member shows "Every team needs at least one member" at once.</p>
        <p>When a row leaves, the form notices: it drops that row's messages and re-checks the whole form. After a submit, that re-check keeps the error summary in line with the shorter list, so a removed row's entry leaves with no second submit.</p>
        <p>A list has no input of its own, so each list rule shows its message through a <code>FormidableCollectionMessage</code> (the message list for a whole list): one above the teams, and one above each team's members.</p>
        <p>The validator puts all four rules among its draft rules (in its <code>ConfigureDraftRules</code>): the rules a draft save checks, which a submit checks too. This page has no draft save, only <strong>Submit</strong>. That grouping decides what a save or a submit checks, not when a message appears. As you edit, the form checks what a submit would, and shows messages only for the fields you have changed.</p>
        <p>The asterisks come from the validator, row by row. Each <code>FormidableRequiredIndicator</code> (the component that draws the mark) reads the same <code>ChildRules</code> that judge the row. The two list rules (at least one team, at least one member) have no label on this page to carry a mark.</p>
    </HowItWorks>
    <Demo>
        <FormidableForm Model="_roster" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid" Options="_options">
            <div class="summary-slot">
                <FormidableSummary />
            </div>
            <FormidableCollectionMessage For="() => _roster.Teams" />

            @* A collection rule fails against the list, not against any one input, so its summary entry
               has nothing to focus unless some element carries the collection's id AND can take focus:
               the container holding every team for the roster's own rule, and each team's box for that
               team's member rule, each with the tabindex a div and a fieldset need to be focusable at
               all. Both ids come from the model via FormidableField's own ElementId, so no page-owned
               state is needed to keep them in step. *@
            <FormidableField For="() => _roster.Teams" Context="teamsField">
                <div class="team-list" id="@teamsField.ElementId" tabindex="-1">
                    @foreach (var team in _roster.Teams)
                    {
                        @* @key="team" is what keeps THIS component bound to this team as the list
                           reorders — without it, Blazor would reuse the component positionally, and a
                           moved row's member actions and message list would stay pointed at whichever
                           team originally rendered in that screen slot. *@
                        <FormidableField For="() => team.Members" Context="membersField" @key="team">
                            <fieldset id="@membersField.ElementId" tabindex="-1">
                                <legend>Team</legend>
                                <div class="field">
                                    <label>Name <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => team.Name" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="team.Name" /></label>
                                    <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => team.Name" />
                                </div>

                                <FormidableCollectionMessage For="() => team.Members" />
                                <ul class="member-list">
                                    @foreach (var member in team.Members)
                                    {
                                        <li class="field" @key="member">
                                            <label>Alias <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => member.Alias" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="member.Alias" /></label>
                                            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => member.Alias" />
                                            <div class="actions">
                                                <button type="button" @onclick="() => membersField.RemoveItem(team.Members, member)">Remove</button>
                                                <button type="button" @onclick="() => membersField.TryEdit(() => MoveUp(team.Members, member))">Move up</button>
                                            </div>
                                        </li>
                                    }
                                </ul>
                                <div class="actions">
                                    <button type="button" @onclick="() => membersField.AddItem(team.Members, new Member())">Add member</button>
                                    <button type="button" @onclick="() => teamsField.RemoveItem(_roster.Teams, team)">Remove team</button>
                                    <button type="button" @onclick="() => teamsField.TryEdit(() => MoveUp(_roster.Teams, team))">Move team up</button>
                                </div>
                            </fieldset>
                        </FormidableField>
                    }
                </div>

                <div class="actions">
                    <button type="button" @onclick="() => teamsField.AddItem(_roster.Teams, new Team())">Add team</button>
                    <button type="submit">Submit</button>
                </div>
            </FormidableField>
        </FormidableForm>

        <p role="status">@_status</p>
    </Demo>
</TeachingPanel>
